\begin_layout Section
\paragraph_spacing single
\noindent
Location
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
FAST is located in Dawodang depression, Kedu Town, Pingtang County, Qiannan
 Buyei and Miao Autonomous Prefecture, Guizhou Province, China.
 This location was chosen to utilize the natural screening effect of the
 terrain to reduce electromagnetic interference, thus providing a clearer
 and more accurate radio astronomical observation environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Structure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Mechanical Structure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main structure of FAST is a huge concave mesh structure, consisting
 of 4,450 precisely controllable reflecting panels, each with a diameter
 of 500 meters, capable of effectively collecting radio signals from the
 sky.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Detector Structure and Materials 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The detector system is mounted in the telescope's focus cabin and can be
 moved precisely in three dimensions via a complex cable system to align
 with different celestial targets.
 The detectors use highly sensitive materials and designs to maximize the
 capture of faint signals from deep space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Focusing Technology 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
FAST employs an innovative multi-beam feed system that can receive radio
 waves from multiple directions simultaneously, greatly enhancing the efficiency
 and flexibility of observations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Detection Wavelength and Sensitivity 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
FAST is designed to cover a wide frequency range from 70 MHz to 3 GHz, enabling
 it to detect a variety of radio sources from galactic cores to distant
 galaxies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Detection Results and Major Discoveries 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Pulsar Detection 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since its operation, FAST has successfully detected over a thousand pulsars,
 a number surpassing the total of other telescopes globally during the same
 period, including a significant number of valuable millisecond pulsars
 and pulsar binaries.
 For instance, the first detected and certified millisecond pulsar by FAST,
 J0318+0253, with a spin period of 5.19 milliseconds and located about 4000
 light-years from Earth, is one of the weakest high-energy millisecond pulsars
 discovered to date.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Fast Radio Bursts (FRBs) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
FAST has also achieved significant success in detecting fast radio bursts,
 which are intense short-duration radio flashes originating from deep space.
 Observations of FRB20201124A revealed three instances of approximately
 90-degree polarization angle jumps, suggesting highly specific conditions,
 possibly special geometries.
 In March 2023, FAST's discovery of repeating fast radio bursts was named
 one of China's top ten scientific advancements of 2022.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Hydrogen Line Observations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By observing the 21-centimeter line of neutral hydrogen, FAST has conducted
 detailed gas mapping of multiple galaxies, data crucial for understanding
 the formation and evolution of galaxies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
FAST is also employed in the search for signals from extraterrestrial civilizati
ons.
 Although no definitive evidence has been found to date, its high sensitivity
 significantly enhances the efficiency and likelihood of detecting such
 signals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Scientific Theoretical Analysis 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The construction and operation of FAST have not only advanced numerous high-tech
 fields and boosted original innovation capabilities but also provided opportuni
ties for discoveries and breakthroughs in fundamental research areas such
 as cosmological large-scale physics, deep structures of matter, and more.
 FAST’s high sensitivity allows it to detect signals from 13.7 billion years
 ago, vital for studying the origins and evolution of the universe.
 Additionally, FAST's observational capabilities provide new perspectives
 and data for cutting-edge scientific questions, such as understanding dark
 matter and dark energy and searching for the first celestial bodies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
International Collaboration and Future Applications 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
FAST has extensive international scientific collaborations with nearly all
 major global radio observatories, fostering substantial and stable partnerships.
 This international collaboration not only accelerates technological innovation
 but also provides a powerful tool for global astronomical research, suggesting
 more possibilities for future deep-space exploration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Conclusion 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The construction and operation of the FAST telescope demonstrate China's
 leadership in large-scale scientific projects, providing a powerful tool
 for global astronomical research and suggesting more possibilities for
 future exploration of the deep universe.
 The successful operation of FAST has not only elevated China's status in
 the international astronomical community but also contributed valuable
 data and theoretical bases for humanity’s understanding of the universe.
\end_layout

